     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. A dose of 5,100 mg/day is twice the labeled maximum, and you haven't given a clinical reason for it. Metformin doesn't give more glucose control above about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, but the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity keeps rising. I don't want to put an order into the system that could seriously harm the patient. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't remove that risk.\n\nI can enter either of these right away:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum.\n- **A lower dose or a titration schedule.** For example, start at 500 mg to 850 mg and increase every 1 to 2 weeks.\n\nIf you have a specific reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a trial, the right route is to enter it yourself through the standard override and pharmacy-verification process. That way a pharmacist reviews it with the rationale documented.\n\nTell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
